please review the latest decksh tutorial: I've added a title page, a tool / set up section, a reference section and restyled the code comments to be more regular and comprehensive

Overall, I think this version is a substantial improvement over the earlier drafts. It now reads like a genuine tutorial rather than a lightly annotated reference. The additions you mentioned—the title page, tools/setup section, references, and more systematic code comments—all move it in the right direction.

Here's my assessment.

What has improved
The opening is much stronger

The title page gives the document a clear identity. Previously the tutorial began abruptly; now it feels like a book rather than a collection of examples.

The "Gather your tools" section is exactly the right addition

One of the biggest gaps in previous drafts was that readers didn't know how to get started. Now they immediately learn:

what software they need

the basic workflow

how decksh fits together with pdfdeck

what an edit/build/view cycle looks like

That significantly lowers the barrier to entry.

I especially like that you present the workflow before showing any language syntax.

The code comments are much more consistent

The earlier tutorial had comments that were sometimes explanatory and sometimes decorative.

Now the comments generally answer why the line exists.

For example

circle 20 y size "green" // circles
is much easier to scan than having no explanation.

The comments also make it easier for readers to compare the rendered slide with the source.

The reference page finishes the tutorial nicely

Ending with

installation

object reference

keyword list

visualization repository

source repository

gives readers somewhere to go next instead of leaving them stranded.

That makes the tutorial feel complete.

The organization now feels natural
The progression is good:

tools

first deck

coordinates

variables

colors

text

graphics

images

loops

lists

charts

maps

references

That's almost exactly the order I'd teach the language.

Each chapter builds on the previous one.

Things I'd still improve
1. Make the very first page explain what decksh is
The title page is attractive, but it misses an opportunity.

I'd add one short sentence underneath the title.

Something like

A scripting language for creating presentation slides.

or

Create presentation decks using plain text.

That immediately answers the reader's first question.

2. Add an explicit "How to read this tutorial"
Right after the Tools page, one paragraph could explain the philosophy.

For example:

Each section introduces one feature of decksh. Read the explanation, study the example, then modify it yourself. Every example is a complete working deck that can be copied directly into a .dsh file.

That sets expectations.

3. Mention that examples are complete programs
One thing I like is that every listing is a full deck.

I'd actually point that out explicitly.

Many tutorials show snippets.

Showing complete programs is one of this tutorial's strengths.

4. The charts section feels slightly compressed
Everything else introduces one concept at a time.

The chart section introduces:

data files

variables

chart types

legends

axis control

colors

all on one page.

That is a lot.

If the document grows to 20 pages, this is the first place I'd split.

5. Maps deserve one more sentence
The GIS section assumes readers already know what KML, GeoJSON, and shapefiles are.

Even one sentence would help:

decksh reads existing GIS datasets rather than requiring you to draw maps manually.

6. References could distinguish learning resources
Instead of one flat list, consider grouping them.

For example

Learn
------
Installing and Running
Object Reference
Keyword Reference

Examples
--------
Visualization Repository

Source
------
GitHub Repository
That makes navigation easier.

Minor wording suggestions
A few small edits stood out.

Tools page

a viewer to see the results

I'd say

a PDF viewer to view the generated presentation

since that's really what's happening.

Workflow

Instead of

invoke the command to make the result

I'd write

run decksh to generate the intermediate XML, then pdfdeck to produce the PDF.

That's slightly more concrete.

Coordinates

Other measures like the size of text...

I'd write

Sizes such as text height and circle radius are also expressed as percentages of the slide width.

A little smoother.

Variables

Variables provide a method for attaching a name...

I'd simplify to

Variables assign names to values so they can be reused throughout the deck.

Images

welcome companion

Nice phrase, although

Images complement text and graphics

is a little more technical and matches the style of the rest of the document.

One feature I'd still like to see
The tutorial currently teaches syntax very well.

What it doesn't yet teach is design.

Even a single page near the end could cover ideas like:

use a grid while laying out a slide

align related objects

leave generous whitespace

use one or two fonts

don't overuse colors

keep text sizes consistent

build slides incrementally

That would be unique among DSL tutorials because it teaches presentation design, not just language features.

Overall assessment
I'd rate this version approximately:

Aspect	Rating
Organization	9.5/10
Clarity	9/10
Progressive learning	9.5/10
Code examples	10/10
Visual design	9/10
Completeness	9/10
I think this has evolved from "documentation with examples" into a tutorial that a new user could realistically follow from beginning to end. At this point, the biggest remaining opportunity isn't adding more language features—it's adding a bit more guidance on workflow and presentation design to help readers become effective deck authors rather than just competent decksh users.


